---
import Guide from "../../../layouts/Guide.astro"
import Code from "../../../components/Code.astro"
import InstallCommand from "./partials/InstallCommand.astro"
import NextSteps from "./partials/NextSteps.astro"
import PickAndChoose from "./partials/PickAndChoose.astro"
import ServerSupport from "./partials/ServerSupport.astro"
---

<Guide overline="Guide">
  <Fragment slot="title">Getting started</Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="preamble">
    OPUI ships first-class React 19 components. Install the package, import what
    you need, and you're set.
  </Fragment>

  <section id="installation">
    <h2>Install via NPM</h2>
    <InstallCommand packages="react@19 react-dom@19" />
  </section>

  <section id="css">
    <h2>Import the CSS</h2>
    <p>
      React components ship markup only - the CSS still has to be imported once,
      in your root layout or the entry file that renders your app. Import
      everything:
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`// src/main.tsx
import "opui-css/css/imports.css"`}
      lang="tsx"
    />
    <p>
      Or pick and choose in a CSS file, and import that file in your entry
      instead:
    </p>
    <PickAndChoose file="src/styles/main.css" />
    <Code
      code={`// src/main.tsx
import "./styles/main.css"`}
      lang="tsx"
    />
  </section>

  <section id="usage">
    <h2>How to use</h2>
    <Code
      code={`import { Button } from "opui-css/react"

export default function App() {
  return <Button variant="filled">Click me</Button>
}`}
      lang="tsx"
    />
    <p>
      Containers take parts, such as <code>CardHeader</code> and
      <code>DialogFooter</code>. Other slots are props: the default slot is
      <code>children</code> and named slots are camelCased, such as
      <code>endText</code>. Form controls take the native <code>value</code> or
      <code>defaultValue</code>, <code>checked</code> or
      <code>defaultChecked</code>, and <code>onChange</code> props.
    </p>
    <NextSteps />
  </section>

  <section id="ssr">
    <h2>Server rendering</h2>
    <p>
      Components that need context, state or effects are marked
      <code>"use client"</code>. The rest also render as React Server
      Components. Without hydration (Astro with <code>@astrojs/react</code> and
      no <code>client:*</code> directive, or <code>renderToString</code> on its
      own), every component renders complete HTML, and the native controls work
      and submit with their form. The props in the last column only update on
      the client, and the server icon next to each says what you see until then
      and how to work around it. The table is read from the component sources
      and their API docs.
    </p>
    <ServerSupport framework="react" />
    <p>
      Hydrate those (<code>client:load</code> in Astro) or render them on the
      client. Component pages say what needs hydration next to the browser
      support chips, and the API tables mark each prop with a crossed-out server
      icon.
    </p>
  </section>
</Guide>
